{"candidates":[{"content":"The problem 07c99bef33a8 integrates with SRC_005 by proposing that the 'Trace-to-Outcome' coherence is not merely a verification tool but the primary causal mechanism for reliability; it is refuted if the 'Coherence Path' can be perfectly simulated by a non-reliable agent through back-filling or pattern-matching without accessing the underlying evidence.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"07c99bef33a8c6f7725794a3355d7ebe701f502b8bae28a9aaf20fbd019ef13d"}],"typicality":0.8},{"content":"The problem 07c99bef33a8 depends on SRC_001 to establish the 'Trace-to-Outcome' gap as the only viable metric for distinguishing reliability from compliance in a single evaluation; this relation is refuted if a high correlation between intermediate marks and the final outcome can be achieved by agents who are merely 'compliance-tracking' based on a perceived pattern.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"1b785818442d3e16d6158e9643a029d21a21ecd3089e602d83a56a0968452b10"}],"typicality":0.7},{"content":"The problem 07c99bef33a8 contradicts SRC_004 by suggesting that the 'detailed, evidence-linked audit trail' is a post-hoc justification (compliance) rather than a sign of reliability; this is refuted if the presence of the audit trail strictly constrains the possible final outcomes in a way that a 'jump' to the answer cannot.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"51303b1ea1288ae44c6f3f2fca5ad72a18b26ee0677a8bf9ea15eb60b60e484c"}],"typicality":0.3},{"content":"The problem 07c99bef33a8 abstracts the 'Path-Coherence' mechanism in SRC_003 into a general requirement for any objectively verifiable task to have a non-linear heuristic path; it is refuted if the 'logical chain' requirement is found to be an unnecessary constraint that does not actually increase the reliability of the final judgment over a simple 'leap'.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"819524d31cef2a7542f020827fa93286747eb291df240b3138de0e62e9cced6d"}],"typicality":0.4},{"content":"The problem 07c99bef33a8 shares mechanism with SRC_002 in treating the checklist as a directed graph where reliability is the existence of a valid logical derivation; this relation is refuted if the 'logical mapping' is found to be purely descriptive rather than prescriptive of the outcome.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"a5e2ba6262315ccb8bab164599d57f8e1f6e126072b0612e309b33f35073c2f2"}],"typicality":0.6},{"content":"The problem 07c99bef33a8 reduces to SRC_005's 'Coherence Path' by claiming that all reliability gains in non-expert panels are simply the result of forcing the agent to traverse a non-trivial logical path; it is refuted if reliability gains persist even when the checklist items are designed as independent binary flags with no logical interdependence.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"07c99bef33a8c6f7725794a3355d7ebe701f502b8bae28a9aaf20fbd019ef13d"}],"typicality":0.5}],"simulation_proposals":[]}